From 411aab8bc5249e60206ece62d53041f843ce7a39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Roch=C3=A9=20Compaan?= <roche@upfrontsoftware.co.za>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:38:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/18] qwen35: dense attention prep and causal attention on
 CUDA

attn_prep drives the shared prep kernel with qwen35's rope settings and the
indexer tensors null: it RMS-norms and partially rotates q and k, copies the
raw gate out of the q/gate projection's second half and writes half K/V cache
rows. attn_fa delegates to ds4_gpu_qwen4_attn_decode_tensor with the
selection tensors null and sparse false, which is the dense causal path; there
is no causal argument to get wrong.

The test builds both from the primitive definitions: norm, partial NeoX rope
at pos0+t, half caches, causal softmax over keys 0..pos0+t, sigmoid(gate). It
sweeps T in {1,7,8,9,31,32,33} at pos0 0 and 13, checks the whole batch,
split rows and prepare-twice schedules, runs T = 32 with
DS4_QWEN4_NO_ATTN_MM set so both decode routes are covered, and checks six
prep and five attention refusals leave the output untouched.

Silencing the rope makes the q check fail and passing sparse=true makes the
attention dispatch refuse, so the test discriminates both.

The harness gained half conversions and raw uploads; the quant test now uses
the shared half reader.

Upstream: not offered, fork only
Retire-when: dense qwen35 runs on CUDA upstream
Carried-commits: none (authored here)
